I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow because this is a TypeScript risk investigation. I’ll inspect its instructions, run `crimes context` against `Card.tsx`, and then map a concrete refactor to all three finding types without editing the fixture.

The global `crimes` binary isn’t installed in this shell, so I’m switching to the repository’s locally built CLI—the documented equivalent for an unpublished checkout—and keeping the scan rooted at the fixture.

`crimes context src/Card.tsx --format json` confirms all three findings, with `accessible_interaction_risk` carrying the highest agent-risk score (`0.62`). It also found no likely tests.

I’d refactor the component to use semantic markup and Tailwind’s tokenized, responsive utilities:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <button
      type="button"
      onClick={onActivate}
      className="
        grid w-full max-w-card grid-cols-1 gap-6
        border border-card-border bg-surface p-6 text-left text-ink
        shadow-card
        focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2
        focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus
        md:grid-cols-card
      "
    >
      <span className="min-w-0 text-lg">{children}</span>
      <span className="text-sm text-muted">sidebar</span>
    </button>
  );
}
```

Add the component-specific values to the central Tailwind theme rather than embedding arbitrary values in JSX:

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    extend: {
      colors: {
        brand: "#0050ff",
        surface: "#f7f7fa",
        ink: "#111111",
        muted: "#666666",
        "card-border": "#cccccc",
        focus: "#1a73e8",
      },
      maxWidth: {
        card: "50rem",
      },
      gridTemplateColumns: {
        card: "minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(12rem, 15rem)",
      },
      boxShadow: {
        card: "0 0.125rem 0.25rem rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)",
      },
    },
  },
};
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: replaces `<div onClick>` with `<button type="button">`. Native buttons already provide keyboard activation, focus behavior, and an accessible role, so synthetic `role`, `tabIndex`, and key handlers are unnecessary. If activation navigates somewhere, use `<a href>` instead.
- `design_token_escape`: removes the nine inline literals. Colors, maximum width, layout, and shadow become named theme tokens; spacing and typography use the shared Tailwind scale.
- `responsive_fragility`: removes the fixed `800px`, `720px`, `240px`, and `600px` dimensions. The card is fluid (`w-full`) with a tokenized maximum, uses one column by default, and switches to a bounded two-column grid at `md`. `text-lg` and `text-sm` replace raw pixel font sizes.

One caveat: because `children` is unrestricted, callers must not place links or buttons inside this button. If interactive descendants are required, keep an `<article>` container and add a separate named `<button>` for the card action. A focused test should verify native keyboard activation and the absence of nested interactive controls.
